---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AffiliateLink from '../../components/AffiliateLink.astro';

const categories = [
  {
    name: 'The ovens',
    notes:
      "We cook on one oven: the Ninja Woodfire. It's the only oven on this page we own. The others are the ones we compared before buying — the notes on them come from manufacturer specs and our research, not from cooking on them. The full comparison is in our guide to the best outdoor pizza ovens under $500.",
    detail: [
      {
        label: 'Ninja Woodfire 8-in-1 Outdoor Oven (OO101) — our oven',
        affiliateId: 'ninja-woodfire-oven',
        body: "Electric, 105–700°F, with a pellet hopper that adds real wood smoke when you want it. Our pizza routine: preheat to 550°F for about 20 minutes, confirm the stone reads around 540°F with an infrared thermometer, par-bake the oiled dough for about 1 minute 30 seconds, then top it and bake about 5 minutes without turning. The crust comes out evenly golden rather than leopard-spotted, since it won't reach Neapolitan temperatures. It also smokes and roasts — we've done pulled pork and chicken on it. After more than a year, no real problems.",
      },
      {
        label: 'Ooni Koda 2 — if Neapolitan heat is the goal (researched, not owned)',
        body: "Propane, 14-inch stone, rated to 950°F, about 35 lbs, $499. The most direct route under $500 to fast, high-heat pizza. It replaced the 12-inch Koda 12, which Ooni now sells as a first-generation model and which has been hard to find in stock.",
      },
      {
        label: 'Ooni Fyra 12 — wood pellets (researched, not owned)',
        body: "Burns hardwood pellets from a gravity-fed hopper. Ooni rates it at 950°F in about 15 minutes, with roughly 60-second pizzas, at about 22 lbs and around $349. Real live-fire flavor, but a pellet fire is something you manage, and wind matters more than with gas or electric.",
      },
      {
        label: 'Solo Stove Pi Prime — propane (researched, not owned)',
        body: "Solo Stove's propane-only oven, rated above 950°F, preheating in about 15 minutes and cooking in as little as 90 seconds. A bit over 30 lbs, fits pizzas up to about 13 inches, and includes a cover. $399.99 list and frequently on sale.",
      },
      {
        label: 'Cuisinart 3-in-1 Pizza Oven Plus — the budget multitasker (researched, not owned)',
        affiliateId: 'cuisinart-3in1-oven',
        body: "Propane, rated to 700°F, with 6–8 minute pizzas per Cuisinart. Swaps between a 13-inch cordierite stone, a griddle, and a grill grate. Heavier than it looks at around 56 lbs, and $249.99 list — the lowest price here.",
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'Pellets',
    notes:
      'Hardwood pellets for the Ninja — they add smoke, not heat. We rotate oak, maple, and a cherry blend. Food-grade pellets only.',
    detail: [
      {
        label: 'Ninja All-Purpose Blend (cherry, maple, oak)',
        affiliateId: 'all-purpose-pellets',
        body: 'The everyday bag. Mild, slightly sweet, works on pizza, chicken, vegetables, fish. If you\'re buying one bag, buy this one. The sweetness disappears into savory food and the smoke level is approachable rather than aggressive.',
      },
      {
        label: 'Ninja Robust Blend (hickory, cherry, maple, oak)',
        affiliateId: 'robust-blend-pellets',
        body: 'The BBQ bag. Pulled pork, brisket, ribs, anything where you want that classic smoke depth. Not for pizza unless you specifically want a campfire note on your crust, which occasionally you do.',
      },
      {
        label: 'Kona variety pack',
        affiliateId: 'kona-pellets',
        body: 'Eight one-pound bags, different single-source woods. Good for experimenting without committing to a full five-pound bag of mesquite only to discover you hate mesquite on pizza. (Most people hate mesquite on pizza.) Food-grade pellets only — heating pellets contain binders not meant to go near food.',
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'Peels',
    notes:
      'A wide perforated aluminum launching peel, plus a small turning peel. Wood looks great but eats flour and warps in wet New England air.',
    detail: [
      {
        label: 'Launching peel',
        affiliateId: 'ninja-peel',
        body: 'Aluminum, perforated, sized to fit the Woodfire opening. The perforations matter — flour falls through instead of burning on the stone. The Ninja XSKOPPL is purpose-built for this oven. A generic 12-inch perforated aluminum peel does the same job for less. Wood peels launch fine but don\'t retrieve as cleanly.',
      },
      {
        label: 'Turning peel',
        body: 'We own a small round one. At our usual 550°F we don\'t rotate pizzas, so it\'s optional — get a good launching peel first.',
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'The dough',
    notes:
      'Three ingredients that actually move the needle on crust quality. Everything else is technique.',
    detail: [
      {
        label: 'Caputo Pizzeria Type "00" flour',
        affiliateId: 'caputo-flour',
        body: 'This is the flour. Finely milled Italian 00 for high-heat baking, low ash content, strong gluten structure that stretches without tearing. The difference between this and all-purpose or even bread flour is not subtle once you\'ve felt the dough properly fermented — it\'s extensible in a way that lets you open the ball by hand without fighting it. Our everyday dough runs it at 65% hydration.',
      },
      {
        label: 'Baleine Fine Sea Salt',
        affiliateId: 'baleine-sea-salt',
        body: 'Salt matters more than most pizza recipes admit. We prefer a clean-tasting sea salt over iodized table salt for flavor. Baleine is a French Atlantic sea salt, fine enough to dissolve fully into the dough without streaking. We use 13 g of salt per 500 g of flour — about 2.6%. Worth weighing rather than guessing.',
      },
      {
        label: 'Caputo Lievito dry yeast',
        affiliateId: 'caputo-yeast',
        body: 'The same Caputo that makes the flour makes a dry yeast formulated for long cold ferments. It performs reliably at refrigerator temperatures over 24–72 hours where regular active dry yeast can stall or produce off flavors. We use 1.5 g per 500 g of flour for a 24–48 hour cold ferment.',
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'The bread maker',
    notes:
      'Dump in the ingredients, press start, come back to finished dough. The Cuisinart CBK-110NAS for anyone who wants consistent results without the hand-mixing — especially useful when pizza night means six or eight balls at once.',
    detail: [
      {
        label: 'Cuisinart CBK-110NAS',
        affiliateId: 'cuisinart-bread-maker',
        body: 'Let\'s be honest about this one first: you don\'t need a bread maker to make great pizza dough. The Caputo 00, Baleine salt, and Caputo Lievito above are an easy hand-mix — combine, rest, fold twice, refrigerate. It takes about 10 minutes of active work. The Cuisinart earns its place for a different reason: consistency at volume. When pizza night means six or eight balls of dough, hand-mixing and portioning that quantity gets tedious. Use the Dough cycle specifically — it mixes and kneads without baking, which is what you want for pizza. The 450-watt motor handles Caputo 00 without straining, though you may need to add flour a teaspoon at a time during the second knead if the dough seems too wet. After a batch or two you\'ll know exactly where it needs to land. Compact footprint, fits under standard cabinets, brushed stainless steel, non-stick pan cleans quickly by hand.',
      },
      {
        label: 'OXO Good Grips Bench Scraper',
        affiliateId: 'oxo-bench-scraper',
        body: 'The most underrated tool on the dough table. Divides dough balls cleanly without deflating them, lifts stuck dough off the stainless top in one pass, and scrapes the board between pies so you\'re not building up flour char through a long pizza night. Costs about $10. If you don\'t own one, buy it before the next session.',
      },
      {
        label: 'Etekcity Digital Kitchen Scale',
        affiliateId: 'etekcity-scale',
        body: 'Already mentioned weighing salt — 13 g per batch — this is the scale we use. Dough by weight is more consistent than dough by volume, full stop. A $15 scale removes one variable from every batch. The Etekcity is accurate, slim, easy to clean, and doesn\'t take up meaningful counter space.',
      },
      {
        label: 'Chef Pomodoro Pizza Dough Proofing Box',
        affiliateId: 'chef-pomodoro-proofing-box',
        body: 'When pizza night means six or eight balls, individual proofing containers keep each ball round and properly shaped through the cold ferment. The Chef Pomodoro box fits 4–6 dough balls, has a carry handle for moving from fridge to patio, and stacks cleanly in the refrigerator. Same logic as the bread maker — not necessary for two pizzas, genuinely useful at volume.',
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'Pizza stone & care',
    notes:
      'The Woodfire ships with a 12-inch square cordierite stone. We let it burn off between pies, brush dry, and never wash with soap. A hot stone is a forgiving stone.',
    detail: [
      {
        label: 'Stone temperature vs. air temperature',
        affiliateId: 'infrared-thermometer',
        body: 'The Woodfire\'s display shows you the air temp inside the oven — not the stone surface. Fully preheated, ours reads about 540°F at our usual 550°F setting and about 695°F on the 700°F setting. A $20 infrared thermometer aimed at the stone tells you when it\'s actually ready. Before we owned one, we\'d preheat for 20 minutes and still occasionally get pale, undercooked bottoms. After: consistent crust, every time.',
      },
      {
        label: 'The patina is the point',
        body: 'A pizza stone darkens over time and that\'s correct. The carbonized layer seasons the stone and helps it perform better. Don\'t scrape it back to white. Don\'t wash it with soap — soap soaks into the porous stone and flavors your next pizza in a way you won\'t enjoy. Scrape off loose debris with a dry brush. For stuck cheese, let the stone heat up fully and burn it off. If you crack your stone, let it cool completely before moving it and replace it before cooking again.',
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'The cart',
    notes:
      'Counter-height, stainless steel top, sealed storage underneath. The Keter Unity is where the dough gets built and the toppings get staged.',
    detail: [
      {
        label: 'Keter Unity Stainless Steel & Resin Outdoor Kitchen Cart',
        affiliateId: 'keter-stand',
        body: 'The piece of gear that changed how pizza night actually flows. Before it, prepping dough and toppings happened in the kitchen, then everything got shuttled outside. Now the whole operation runs outdoors — dough gets opened on the stainless top, toppings go in small bowls on the lower shelf, peels hang on the hooks, pellet bags live in the sealed cabinet underneath. The stainless top wipes clean between pies. Counter height matches a standard kitchen counter. The sealed storage cabinet keeps pellets and flour dry through New England rain. The wheels lock, which matters when you\'re pushing a peel under a pizza. It\'s not the official Ninja stand — the XSKUNSTAND fits the oven precisely and is the right answer if you want everything matched. But the Keter gives you a full prep station rather than just an oven stand, and for how we run pizza night the dedicated counter space has been worth more than the perfect oven fit.',
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'The cover',
    notes:
      'The Woodfire is weather-resistant. New England isn\'t. Get the cover.',
    detail: [
      {
        label: 'Ninja XSKOCVR',
        affiliateId: 'ninja-cover',
        body: 'The power cord is short and the electronics are real — this isn\'t a cast iron grate you can leave out through a February nor\'easter. The official Ninja XSKOCVR fits exactly right. Third-party covers sized specifically for the OO100 series run about half the price and hold up through rain and wind without issue. If you have the Keter cart next to the oven as a permanent setup, size your cover for the oven alone — the Keter\'s stainless top and resin body handle weather without any help.',
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'The cord',
    notes:
      'The Woodfire ships with a 4.5-foot cord. Almost everyone discovers this the hard way.',
    detail: [
      {
        label: '12-gauge outdoor-rated extension cord',
        affiliateId: 'extension-cord',
        body: 'Get at least 15 feet. The gauge matters — the oven pulls real wattage and a thin indoor extension cord can overheat. The Keter cart is on wheels, which helps with positioning, but you\'ll still want the cord length to give you flexibility on where the whole setup lands. This is the one thing we\'d buy before the oven arrives.',
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Gear"
  description="Honest notes on the Ninja Woodfire we cook on, the ovens we compared before buying, and the tools and ingredients that keep pizza night running — from Caputo 00 flour to the Keter prep cart."
>
  <section class="container-page py-16 md:py-20">
    <p class="eyebrow">Gear</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 font-display text-4xl text-balance md:text-5xl">What we actually use</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-pretty text-timber-100/85">
      Honest notes on the tools and ingredients that earn their place on the
      deck — the Ninja Woodfire mainly, and the small stack of things that keep
      it running well. Everything here that we own has spent real time on our deck; the other ovens are clearly marked as researched.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-sm text-timber-300/80">
      This page contains affiliate links. If you click and buy something, we may earn a small commission at no extra cost to you.
    </p>

    <ul class="mt-12 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
      {
        categories.map((c) => (
          <li class="card">
            <h2 class="font-display text-2xl text-timber-50">{c.name}</h2>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-timber-100/85">{c.notes}</p>
            {c.detail && (
              <div class="mt-5 space-y-5 border-t border-timber-700/40 pt-5">
                {c.detail.map((d) => (
                  <div>
                    <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-timber-50">{d.label}</p>
                    <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-timber-100/75 leading-relaxed">{d.body}</p>
                    {d.affiliateId && (
                      <p class="mt-2 text-sm">
                        <AffiliateLink id={d.affiliateId} />
                      </p>
                    )}
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>
            )}
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <p class="mt-12 max-w-2xl text-sm text-timber-300/80">
      If something ends up here, it's because we'd recommend it to a friend
      without thinking twice. This page gets updated when something earns
      it — or gets cut.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-sm text-timber-300/80">
      Shopping for someone else's Ninja? Our
      <a href="/blog/gifts-for-ninja-woodfire-owners/" class="underline">gift guide for Ninja Woodfire owners</a>
      is the short list.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
